  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows dry weather across\neast central Florida. GOES-19 satellite imagery and local\nobservations indicate partly sunny sunny skies across east \ncentral Florida. Temperatures are currently in the upper 60s to\nmid 70s with dew points in the upper 40s to upper 50s. Analysis\ncharts show high pressure (~1032mb) building to the north over \nthe north central CONUS to Midwest. Rain chances are expected to\nincrease beginning tonight with isolated showers forecast. Expect\nseasonable to below normal lows in the low 50s to mid 60s. \n\nMonday-Tuesday... Rain chances increase on Monday and Tuesday as\nflow shifts northeast and as a shortwave trough tracks across the\nDeep South (including northern Florida) on Tuesday. Isolated to \nscattered onshore moving showers are forecast on Monday before \nrain chances (~40-80%) increase on Tuesday as moisture gradually \nincreases with persistent onshore flow. There is a Marginal Risk \n(level 1 out of 5) for excessive rainfall Tuesday. The primary \nhazards will be showers capable of wind gusts to 30-40mph and \nheavy rainfall (isolated amounts up to 2-4\"+ total on Tuesday). Do\nnot drive through flooded roadways if you encounter flooding! \nWinds will be breezy at times from the northeast at 10-20mph with \ngusts up to 25-30mph. Expect seasonable to below normal highs in \nthe low to upper 70s. Lows in the low 50s to mid 60s are forecast \nto increase to the upper 50s to low 70s Tuesday.\n\nWednesday-Saturday... Guidance has come into better agreement on\nthe forecast until Friday. However, there are still differences \nin the development and track of a low that is expected to develop \nover the western Atlantic waters which will greatly influence how \nmuch rain east central Florida receives, as well as the coastal \nimpacts for east central Florida. The deterministic models show \na low developing over the southwest Atlantic before tracking north\nor northeast. A low track to the north across the Florida\npeninsulainstead of northeast across the western Atlantic waters\nwould increase the impacts for east central Florida and vice \nversa. Additionally, the LREF-ensemble clusters show a 500mb \ntrough deepening over the western Atlantic and the state of \nFlorida mid to late week which is further south-west than previous\nmodel runs which suggests that the threat for heavy rainfall and \ncoastal impacts has increased for east central Florida. Scattered \nto numerous/widespread off and on onshore moving showers are \nforecast each day before rain chances gradually decrease into the \nweekend. There is at least a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for \nexcessive rainfall Wednesday and Thursday. The potential exists \nfor flooding of roadways and low- lying areas with amounts to \n2-4\" (isolated amounts to 5-7\"+ on Wednesday and Thursday). Winds \nwill be breezy to windy from the northeast before decreasing into \nthe weekend. Seasonable temperatures are forecast.",
